1
00:00:00,170 --> 00:00:02,670
In this video, we're
going to talk more

2
00:00:02,670 --> 00:00:04,545
specifically about
embedded Linux

3
00:00:04,545 --> 00:00:06,330
and also about emulating

4
00:00:06,330 --> 00:00:08,835
embedded Linux
platforms with QEMU.

5
00:00:08,835 --> 00:00:11,190
Learning objectives,
we want to understand

6
00:00:11,190 --> 00:00:13,125
the different elements
of embedded Linux.

7
00:00:13,125 --> 00:00:14,490
Talk a little bit more about

8
00:00:14,490 --> 00:00:17,085
Linux hardware support and
what it means for embedded,

9
00:00:17,085 --> 00:00:21,210
and introduce QEMU for emulation
of embedded platforms.

10
00:00:21,210 --> 00:00:23,775
The elements of embedded Linux,

11
00:00:23,775 --> 00:00:25,290
the first one is the toolchain,

12
00:00:25,290 --> 00:00:27,090
that's the compiler linker

13
00:00:27,090 --> 00:00:28,290
all the different
tools that we've

14
00:00:28,290 --> 00:00:31,770
talked about already in previous
weeks and for instance,

15
00:00:31,770 --> 00:00:33,450
the cross-compiler
that we set up

16
00:00:33,450 --> 00:00:36,660
in week 2 in assignment 2.

17
00:00:36,660 --> 00:00:38,220
This is everything that
you need to create

18
00:00:38,220 --> 00:00:40,105
code for your specific device.

19
00:00:40,105 --> 00:00:42,695
There's the bootloader that's
going to be initializing

20
00:00:42,695 --> 00:00:45,560
the board and loading the
initial Linux kernel.

21
00:00:45,560 --> 00:00:48,350
Then there's a kernel that
is going to be managing

22
00:00:48,350 --> 00:00:51,820
system resources and all the
interface with hardware.

23
00:00:51,820 --> 00:00:53,710
Finally, there's the
root filesystem,

24
00:00:53,710 --> 00:00:54,770
which is where all of the

25
00:00:54,770 --> 00:00:56,780
userspace content's
going to exist.

26
00:00:56,780 --> 00:00:58,340
Libraries, the programs,

27
00:00:58,340 --> 00:00:59,510
everything that runs after

28
00:00:59,510 --> 00:01:01,670
the kernel completes
initialization,

29
00:01:01,670 --> 00:01:04,000
it's going to live in
the root filesystem.

30
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:06,800
Linux, in terms of
hardware support,

31
00:01:06,800 --> 00:01:09,325
it supports 30 plus
architectures today.

32
00:01:09,325 --> 00:01:12,505
All the architectures today
are at least 32 bits,

33
00:01:12,505 --> 00:01:15,905
most of them include a
Memory Management Unit, MMU.

34
00:01:15,905 --> 00:01:17,135
There is an exception in

35
00:01:17,135 --> 00:01:20,285
MicroClinux where you
can run without an MMU,

36
00:01:20,285 --> 00:01:22,430
but it's pretty common to have

37
00:01:22,430 --> 00:01:25,465
MMU and any hardware
that would run Linux

38
00:01:25,465 --> 00:01:27,750
today and that hardware

39
00:01:27,750 --> 00:01:31,070
continues to get cheaper and
cheaper as time goes on,

40
00:01:31,070 --> 00:01:34,195
so it's easier to support
Linux requirements.

41
00:01:34,195 --> 00:01:37,340
It does require a large
amount of RAM than maybe what

42
00:01:37,340 --> 00:01:40,850
you've worked on with really
simple microcontroller.

43
00:01:40,850 --> 00:01:43,940
So 16 megabytes is a pretty
good minimum for RAM,

44
00:01:43,940 --> 00:01:45,770
it might be possible to
use the less than that,

45
00:01:45,770 --> 00:01:48,835
but this is a typical value.

46
00:01:48,835 --> 00:01:51,050
It also is going to
require some type of

47
00:01:51,050 --> 00:01:54,620
non-volatile storage and
at least eight megabytes

48
00:01:54,620 --> 00:01:56,000
is probably a good suggestion.

49
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:58,940
Again, you could get less
than that if you tried,

50
00:01:58,940 --> 00:02:01,220
but that's probably around

51
00:02:01,220 --> 00:02:03,125
a lower bar that you
want to look for.

52
00:02:03,125 --> 00:02:05,210
Unfortunately, both of
those are pretty easy

53
00:02:05,210 --> 00:02:07,400
to meet these days too,

54
00:02:07,400 --> 00:02:09,380
hardware is getting
powerful enough that those

55
00:02:09,380 --> 00:02:12,330
are not really high
bars to clear anymore.

56
00:02:12,740 --> 00:02:16,280
If we want to talk about using

57
00:02:16,280 --> 00:02:18,110
something that
emulate hardware that

58
00:02:18,110 --> 00:02:20,120
can have some benefits because,

59
00:02:20,120 --> 00:02:22,490
for instance, we can
start developing

60
00:02:22,490 --> 00:02:25,310
before hardware is
even available.

61
00:02:25,310 --> 00:02:28,850
We can also do things like test

62
00:02:28,850 --> 00:02:32,270
or run on systems that aren't
really hardware devices,

63
00:02:32,270 --> 00:02:34,550
so this could make
our lives easier

64
00:02:34,550 --> 00:02:37,210
in terms of validating
the software that

65
00:02:37,210 --> 00:02:40,270
we write and decoupling some

66
00:02:40,270 --> 00:02:41,380
of the software rewrite from

67
00:02:41,380 --> 00:02:44,100
hardware limitations
and requirements.

68
00:02:44,100 --> 00:02:46,750
A tool that we're going to use

69
00:02:46,750 --> 00:02:49,105
in this class for
emulation is called QEMU.

70
00:02:49,105 --> 00:02:53,304
QEMU emulates a specific
product architecture,

71
00:02:53,304 --> 00:02:55,180
and it can even emulate
a specific board

72
00:02:55,180 --> 00:02:57,140
that uses that architecture.

73
00:02:57,140 --> 00:02:59,450
For instance, qemu-system-arm

74
00:02:59,450 --> 00:03:01,885
simulates ARM
architecture devices,

75
00:03:01,885 --> 00:03:04,135
which is what we're going
to focus on in this class,

76
00:03:04,135 --> 00:03:06,310
and is also a
common architecture

77
00:03:06,310 --> 00:03:09,035
for some of the lower-cost
Linux platforms.

78
00:03:09,035 --> 00:03:12,265
Here's an example of
how you would start

79
00:03:12,265 --> 00:03:15,505
an ARM QEMU system
using qemu-system-arm.

80
00:03:15,505 --> 00:03:16,570
We'll go through each one of

81
00:03:16,570 --> 00:03:19,125
these arguments in later slides.

82
00:03:19,125 --> 00:03:21,915
QEMU system or QEMU in general

83
00:03:21,915 --> 00:03:25,700
uses command-line
arguments for most of the,

84
00:03:25,700 --> 00:03:28,075
that's pretty much the
way that you invoke it.

85
00:03:28,075 --> 00:03:30,780
Sometimes this can get
a little intimidating,

86
00:03:30,780 --> 00:03:31,910
but if you go
through and piece by

87
00:03:31,910 --> 00:03:33,545
piece and look through
the documentation,

88
00:03:33,545 --> 00:03:35,710
it's pretty easy to figure
out what it's doing.

89
00:03:35,710 --> 00:03:38,480
If we look at the -m 256,

90
00:03:38,480 --> 00:03:40,760
we're saying we're emulating

91
00:03:40,760 --> 00:03:43,955
a board that has 256
megabytes of RAM on it.

92
00:03:43,955 --> 00:03:47,740
Then if we use -M versatilepb,

93
00:03:47,740 --> 00:03:49,850
what this means is
that we're emulating

94
00:03:49,850 --> 00:03:53,255
a specific development board
forearm called versatilepb.

95
00:03:53,255 --> 00:03:55,310
The reason we are
using this is just

96
00:03:55,310 --> 00:03:57,080
because it aligns with

97
00:03:57,080 --> 00:04:00,830
the mastering embedded
Linux programming concept

98
00:04:00,830 --> 00:04:03,685
that we're using for
assignment number 3.

99
00:04:03,685 --> 00:04:07,970
The next couple of arguments
are related to how we load

100
00:04:07,970 --> 00:04:11,480
the kernel and how we tell
QEMU to load the kernel.

101
00:04:11,480 --> 00:04:13,010
If you remember we mentioned

102
00:04:13,010 --> 00:04:14,900
before that there's typically

103
00:04:14,900 --> 00:04:17,090
a bootloader as one of

104
00:04:17,090 --> 00:04:19,945
the components of your
embedded system toolchain,

105
00:04:19,945 --> 00:04:21,530
what QEMU allows us to do is

106
00:04:21,530 --> 00:04:23,390
essentially bypass
the bootloader.

107
00:04:23,390 --> 00:04:25,220
Rather than having to go through

108
00:04:25,220 --> 00:04:27,250
a bootloader to
bring up the system,

109
00:04:27,250 --> 00:04:29,115
with QEMU, we can just tell it,

110
00:04:29,115 --> 00:04:30,645
"Start with this kernel image."

111
00:04:30,645 --> 00:04:32,625
That's what we're doing
here with this command.

112
00:04:32,625 --> 00:04:35,720
We're saying this zImage
will be the image that

113
00:04:35,720 --> 00:04:38,975
we built from source
of our kernel image,

114
00:04:38,975 --> 00:04:41,755
so we're telling QEMU to
boot with that image.

115
00:04:41,755 --> 00:04:46,025
Then this -append line is
what we're going to pass

116
00:04:46,025 --> 00:04:47,930
into the kernel once
it's loaded into

117
00:04:47,930 --> 00:04:50,030
memory to tell the
kernel how to start.

118
00:04:50,030 --> 00:04:52,415
This is telling where the
console is going to be,

119
00:04:52,415 --> 00:04:53,975
where the user is
going to interact,

120
00:04:53,975 --> 00:04:55,945
where the login prompt
going to be shown.

121
00:04:55,945 --> 00:04:58,735
This init is telling

122
00:04:58,735 --> 00:05:02,895
what application to run
as an initialization.

123
00:05:02,895 --> 00:05:08,000
In this case, bin/sh is just
going to give us a login

124
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:11,870
through a shell
script login tunnel

125
00:05:11,870 --> 00:05:14,135
that we can use to
enter shell commands.

126
00:05:14,135 --> 00:05:17,719
Then dtb is describing

127
00:05:17,719 --> 00:05:20,780
how the kernel is
connected to hardware,

128
00:05:20,780 --> 00:05:22,385
and in this case,
it's virtual hardware

129
00:05:22,385 --> 00:05:23,845
because we're emulating it.

130
00:05:23,845 --> 00:05:26,600
We'll get into more
information about this,

131
00:05:26,600 --> 00:05:27,820
what's called the device tree.

132
00:05:27,820 --> 00:05:29,060
We'll get into more information

133
00:05:29,060 --> 00:05:30,020
about that especially later

134
00:05:30,020 --> 00:05:33,240
in the semester when we start
dealing with real hardware.

135
00:05:33,400 --> 00:05:37,355
Then finally, the
last parameter here

136
00:05:37,355 --> 00:05:40,280
is telling QEMU where

137
00:05:40,280 --> 00:05:42,170
the root file system
is coming from.

138
00:05:42,170 --> 00:05:44,240
With the -initrd option,

139
00:05:44,240 --> 00:05:46,190
we're going to use
a root filesystem

140
00:05:46,190 --> 00:05:47,645
in what's called a ramdisk.

141
00:05:47,645 --> 00:05:49,775
In other words,
we're going to take

142
00:05:49,775 --> 00:05:52,700
content that's
wrapped up in what's

143
00:05:52,700 --> 00:05:55,280
called a cpio file and
we're going to extract

144
00:05:55,280 --> 00:05:56,480
that into RAM and we're going to

145
00:05:56,480 --> 00:05:58,860
use that as a root filesystem.